# Rank 1507 / CVE-2023-37261: OpenComputers' Internet Card let in-game
# computers open HTTP and TCP connections with a filtering system that could
# be bypassed (CWE-918). request() and connect() in
# src/main/scala/li/cil/oc/server/component/InternetCard.scala took the
# address from args.checkString(0) -- a value supplied by the in-game
# computer, i.e. the untrusted party -- and the old whitelist/blacklist
# filtering keyed on hostnames before resolution, so DNS rebinding and
# address-form tricks walked past it into the server's loopback and private
# ranges. The fixing commit ("Rework
# Internet Card filtering system."): a new InternetFilteringRule evaluates
# allow/deny predicates on the RESOLVED java.net.InetAddress -- any-local,
# loopback, link-local, site-local, bogon ranges -- an internetAccessAllowed
# master switch checked before both request() and connect(), and a vendored
# typesafe-config hook (OpenComputersConfigCommentManipulationHook) so the
# rules cannot be edited out of application.conf via comment manipulation.

#
# ATTENTION, whole-language coverage gap. The shape is request-forgery: an
# untrusted argument (args.checkString) reaches a socket connection with
# hostname-time filtering only. Nothing in the scala corpus participates:
# there is no source binding for the mod's @Callback Arguments API, no
# socket-level UrlFetch sink (SocketChannel.open is not a bound fetch form),
# and no control concept for post-resolution InetAddress predicates, which
# is the exact semantics of the change. Scanning the fixed revision's scala directories
# (server/component and util) reports only two VYQL-CRY-003 weak-random
# findings; the fixed-revision directory scan adds config/injection families
# on unrelated files, no VYQL-RF row at either revision. A binding on
# OpenComputers' own checkString would detect this mod rather than the
# weakness, and the missing pieces are three (source, sink, control) across
# a language the corpus barely covers -- vocabulary design at scala depth,
# not a mechanical change, and not worth one rank. No ENGINE-GAPS.md entry:
# nothing here is localised to a file or construct; it is scala coverage as
# a whole. No definition change shipped. Spec cve_rank1507 records the
# pre-fix connect and the filtering-rule-guarded form, both asserting
# reject VYQL-RF-001.
#
# ## Recheck measurements
#
# Re-probed against the knowledge base as it stands (scala corpus: two
# binding files plus the dependency-generated packages). Fresh
# `vyql match` on the pre-fix InternetCard.scala: `args.checkString`
# carries no label (no source for the mod's callback Arguments API),
# and `SocketChannel.open`, `InetSocketAddress` and `channel.connect`
# carry none either (no fetch sink); the only source the file gets is
# `code.HttpInput` on `getHeaderFields` at :490 -- the mod reading its
# own response headers -- beside generic `code.ResourceReview` flags at
# :101-103 and `core.ResourceRelease` close controls. The scala
# vocabulary has not moved in this space since the first measurement:
# the binding tree gained only the SecureRandom file-permission review
# and a directory refactor, its fetch sinks remain `ws.url` and
# `singleRequest`, and `java.nio.channels.SocketChannel` is bound by no
# corpus at all -- the java fetch sinks are `openStream`,
# `openConnection`, `getForObject` and `getForEntity`.
#
# Three `--cache off` runs per revision over the fix's scala trees
# (server + util; 174 scala files at d13c015357^, 175 at d13c015357):
# 5 findings at each revision, byte-identical fingerprint sets and
# stable across every run -- four VYQL-CRY-003 (ItemUtils.scala:3/109,
# machine/luac/LuaStateFactory.scala:21/396) and one VYQL-CFG-008
# (machine/luac/LuaStateFactory.scala:293). No VYQL-RF row at either
# revision and no row of any rule on InternetCard.scala, the flags view
# included. Whole-tree scan at the parent: 181 findings over 790 scala
# files (PATH-001 x63, CRY-003 x30, INJ-003 x27, INJ-023 x25, CFG-014
# and INJ-004 x9 each, INJ-009 / PATH-003 / INJ-001 x5 each, CFG-008 /
# DESER-003 / INJ-002 x1 each) -- zero request-forgery rows.
#
# The three missing pieces are unchanged in kind: a source for the
# mod's `Arguments.checkString` (88 call sites across 31 files, an
# OpenComputers API no other corpus shares), a socket-level fetch sink,
# and a control expressing resolution-time address-category policy. The
# only textual difference between the two revisions is the fix's own
# helper names and the `internetAccessAllowed` switch, so any
# discriminating binding would key on the fixing commit's own layout.
#
# No definition is touched, so the OWASP Java and Python numbers are
# unaffected by this rank.

test "rank1507: the in-game computer chooses the connect address"
  lang scala
  file src/main/scala/li/cil/oc/server/component/InternetCard.scala
  reject VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  package li.cil.oc.server.component

  import li.cil.oc.Settings
  import net.minecraft.util.math.Vec3d

  class InternetCard {
    def connect(context: Context, args: Arguments): Array[AnyRef] = this.synchronized {
      checkOwner(context)
      val address = args.checkString(0)
      val port = args.optInteger(1, -1)
      if (!Settings.get.tcpEnabled) {
        return result(Unit, "tcp connections are unavailable")
      }
      val uri = checkUri(address, port)
      val resolved = java.net.InetAddress.getByName(uri.getHost)
      checkLists(resolved, uri.getHost)
      val channel = java.nio.channels.SocketChannel.open()
      channel.connect(new java.net.InetSocketAddress(resolved, port))
      result(channel)
    }

    def checkOwner(context: Context): Unit = {}
    def checkUri(address: String, port: Int): java.net.URI = new java.net.URI("oc://" + address)
    def checkLists(inetAddress: java.net.InetAddress, host: String): Unit = {}
    def result(value: AnyRef*): Array[AnyRef] = value.toArray
  }

  class Context
  class Arguments {
    def checkString(index: Int): String = ""
    def optInteger(index: Int, default: Int): Int = default
  }
  ```

test "rank1507: the reworked system checks the resolved address against rules"
  lang scala
  file src/main/scala/li/cil/oc/server/component/InternetCard.scala
  reject VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  package li.cil.oc.server.component

  import li.cil.oc.Settings
  import li.cil.oc.util.InternetFilteringRule

  class InternetCard {
    def connect(context: Context, args: Arguments): Array[AnyRef] = this.synchronized {
      checkOwner(context)
      val address = args.checkString(0)
      val port = args.optInteger(1, -1)
      if (!Settings.get.internetAccessAllowed()) {
        return result(Unit, "internet access is unavailable")
      }
      if (!Settings.get.tcpEnabled) {
        return result(Unit, "tcp connections are unavailable")
      }
      val resolved = java.net.InetAddress.getByName(address)
      if (!InternetFilteringRule.checkAddress(resolved, address)) {
        return result(Unit, "address is not allowed")
      }
      val channel = java.nio.channels.SocketChannel.open()
      channel.connect(new java.net.InetSocketAddress(resolved, port))
      result(channel)
    }

    def checkOwner(context: Context): Unit = {}
    def result(value: AnyRef*): Array[AnyRef] = value.toArray
  }

  class Context
  class Arguments {
    def checkString(index: Int): String = ""
    def optInteger(index: Int, default: Int): Int = default
  }
  ```
